Great. You've covered **95% of Generics**. The remaining 5% consists of **small but frequently asked interview points**. Here are the important ones with short justifications.

---

# 1. Can Constructors be Generic?

✅ **Yes**, but only if the class itself is generic or non-generic. Constructors can also declare their own type parameter.

```java
class Demo<T>{

    <E> Demo(E value){

        System.out.println(value);

    }
}
```

### Why?

A constructor can perform operations on different datatypes independent of the class's generic type.

---

# 2. Can Static Methods Use Class Type Parameter?

❌ No

```java
class Demo<T>{

    // Compile Error
    static void show(T value){

    }

}
```

### Why?

`T` belongs to the object (instance).

Static members belong to the class.

Static methods execute without creating an object, so `T` is unknown.

---

## Correct Way

```java
class Demo<T>{

    static <E> void show(E value){

        System.out.println(value);

    }

}
```

Here `E` belongs to the method, not the class.

---

# 3. Can Static Variables be Generic?

❌ No

```java
class Demo<T>{

    // Compile Error
    static T value;

}
```

### Why?

One static variable is shared by all objects.

But different objects may use different generic types.

```java
Demo<String>

Demo<Integer>
```

Which datatype should the single static variable have?

Impossible.

---

# 4. Can Generic Classes Extend Another Generic Class?

✅ Yes

```java
class Parent<T>{

}

class Child<T> extends Parent<T>{

}
```

---

Or

```java
class Parent<T>{

}

class Child extends Parent<String>{

}
```

Perfectly valid.

---

# 5. Can Generic Interfaces be Implemented?

✅ Yes

```java
interface Printer<T>{

}

class MyPrinter implements Printer<String>{

}
```

---

# 6. Generic Class Can Have Multiple Type Parameters

```java
class Demo<T,E,K,V>{

}
```

No restriction on the number.

---

# 7. Can We Use Primitive Types?

❌ No

```java
List<int>
```

Compile Error

### Why?

Generics work only with objects.

Use Wrapper Classes.

```java
List<Integer>

List<Double>

List<Character>
```

---

# 8. Diamond Operator (`<>`)

Before Java 7

```java
Box<String> box =
new Box<String>();
```

After Java 7

```java
Box<String> box =
new Box<>();
```

### Why?

Compiler already knows the left side datatype.

No need to repeat it.

---

# 9. Raw Type

```java
Box box = new Box();
```

Called

```text
Raw Type
```

### Why avoid it?

Compiler loses Generic information.

```java
box.set("Java");

box.set(100);
```

Both allowed.

Warnings generated.

---

# 10. Generic Arrays

❌ Illegal

```java
new T[10]
```

### Why?

Arrays require runtime type information.

Generics disappear after Type Erasure.

---

# 11. Generic Exceptions

❌ Illegal

```java
class MyException<T> extends Exception{

}
```

### Why?

Exception handling happens at runtime.

Generics don't exist at runtime.

---

# 12. Can We Catch Generic Exceptions?

❌ No

```java
catch(T e)
```

Compile Error.

---

# 13. Generic Enums

❌ Illegal

```java
enum Demo<T>{

}
```

Enums cannot declare type parameters.

---

# 14. Generic Anonymous Class

✅ Allowed

```java
Box<String> box =
new Box<String>(){

};
```

---

# 15. Generic Lambda

Lambdas cannot declare their own type parameters.

❌

```java
<T> () -> {}
```

But lambdas can implement generic functional interfaces.

```java
Predicate<String> p =
s -> s.isEmpty();
```

---

# 16. Generic Records (Java 16+)

✅ Allowed

```java
record Box<T>(T value){

}
```

---

# 17. instanceof with Parameterized Type

❌ Illegal

```java
if(list instanceof List<String>)
```

Compile Error.

### Why?

After Type Erasure

Only

```text
List
```

exists.

Correct

```java
if(list instanceof List)
```

---

# 18. Method Overloading with Generics

Illegal

```java
void show(List<String> list){

}

void show(List<Integer> list){

}
```

Compile Error

### Why?

After Type Erasure

Both become

```java
show(List list)
```

Duplicate method.

---

# 19. Generic Methods Can Infer Type

```java
<T> void show(T value)
```

Call

```java
show("Java");
```

Compiler automatically infers

```text
T = String
```

No need

```java
<String>show("Java");
```

---

# 20. Type Parameter Naming Convention

Not mandatory.

Convention only.

| Name | Meaning     |
| ---- | ----------- |
| T    | Type        |
| E    | Element     |
| K    | Key         |
| V    | Value       |
| N    | Number      |
| R    | Return Type |

You can even write

```java
class Demo<Apple>{

}
```

Works perfectly.

---

# 21. Generic Methods vs Generic Classes

```java
class Box<T>{

}
```

Whole class uses `T`.

---

```java
class Demo{

    <T> void show(T value){

    }

}
```

Only the method is generic.

---

# 22. Why Java Uses Type Erasure?

To maintain **backward compatibility**.

Java 5 introduced Generics.

Older `.class` files should continue to work without modifying the JVM.

---

# 23. Can We Instantiate a Type Parameter?

❌

```java
new T()
```

### Why?

Compiler doesn't know which constructor to call after Type Erasure.

---

# 24. Can We Access `T.class`?

❌

```java
T.class
```

No runtime type exists.

---

# 25. Bounded Type Erasure

```java
<T extends Number>
```

After Type Erasure

```java
Number
```

Not

```java
Object
```

